WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.750
You’ve probably had a game like this—somebody
starts to do some nutty stuff surprisingly

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:10.870
early in the game, really going ham with their
pieces of cardboard, and by the time they’ve

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:15.339
achieved a decisive win a handful of turns
later, one or more other players are sitting

00:00:15.339 --> 00:00:19.830
dejectedly and grumbling to themselves that
this matchup was unfair.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:24.050
Power level differences within a table can
be a tricky thing to navigate, you know, it’s

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:28.400
not so fun if a game ends up being an absolute
blowout, with some players feeling like they

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.529
didn’t even get a chance, and I’d generally
encourage trying to coordinate power levels

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:33.899
to some degree.

00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:37.629
But, do you wanna know a secret?

00:00:37.629 --> 00:00:42.550
In most cases like this, the relative power
levels of the decks aren’t even the main

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.219
issue.

00:00:44.219 --> 00:00:48.620
Decks with varied power levels can work fine
in the same pod, and games can avoid this

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:54.469
“blowout” issue, but this requires a couple
things to be true, and the first one of these

00:00:54.469 --> 00:00:58.140
things is very simple: decks should run interaction.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:02.989
I mentioned in my last video how a key table
dynamic that keeps powerful decks in check

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:08.380
in a multiplayer format is the ability for
multiple other players to trade cards one

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:11.080
for one with a player who is ahead.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:14.930
And I don’t think I need to say much else
to make clear the problem with a lot of casual

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:19.540
EDH tables—they can’t do this because
they don’t run enough ways to trade cards

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:20.880
like.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.350
A lot of decks throw in some mid-single-digits
number of generic removal spells and/or counter

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:30.570
spells, and then call it good, spending the
rest of their deck on their engines and synergies

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:35.350
and whatnot, building up their deck’s ability
to function against no opponents.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:40.830
Then, when a pod of these decks show up to
a table, before too long a player will naturally

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:45.430
start to get ahead, and their opponents won’t
have a lot of recourse for it.

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:49.460
This amplifies power differences, because
if a deck is even a little bit better at what

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:54.930
it does, say, generating an extra half of
a card per turn cycle in value, there will

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:59.619
be nothing to prevent that advantage from
piling up at an interaction-less table.

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:04.540
A compounding factor for this sort of dynamic
is when decks have high variance.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:08.440
I think the best way to quantify this is to
think about the difference between the deck’s

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:13.379
75th percentile situation, when it’s playing
somewhat better than average, and the deck’s

00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:18.349
25th percentile situation, when it’s playing
somewhat worse than average.

00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:23.110
Most well-constructed decks will have some
difference between these two, but not a massive

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:27.720
one—your 25th percentile situation won’t
feel great, but you’ll still have plays

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.640
you’re setting up for and goals you’re
making progress toward, and maybe you’re

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:36.250
just playing a bit slower, a bit riskier,
or a bit less interactive than usual.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:41.930
By contrast, a lot of casual EDH decks I’ve
seen were built putting so much emphasis on

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:49.640
the ideal scenario, the big pop off factor,
that as much as 25% or 30% or even 40% of

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:53.959
the time they fail to perform meaningful game
actions.

00:02:53.959 --> 00:03:00.030
This comes down to one thing above all others—putting
in too many payoff cards and not enough building

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:01.580
blocks to get there.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:05.569
I’d categorize payoff cards into two types.

00:03:05.569 --> 00:03:10.020
One is amplification cards—cards that convert
your tokens into more tokens, your counters

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:13.900
into more counters, your damage into more
damage, your card draw into more card draw,

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:14.900
and so on.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:18.620
Some number of these cards can be decent for
a deck, especially cards that can rapidly

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:23.269
lead to a win or which synergize with a type
of card you’ll have especially ample access

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:24.269
to.

00:03:24.269 --> 00:03:29.921
However, if you run too many of these and
too few things to be amplified, your game

00:03:29.921 --> 00:03:31.940
plan will be fragile.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:37.830
A while back I had a series of games with
a Zaxara player who would play multiple counter-doublers

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:44.030
and mana-doublers and various other doublers
followed by a single premium hydra, and maybe

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.560
a second one if they got lucky.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.930
If that hydra had survived a turn cycle they
probably would have smacked down the table

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:56.330
in short order, but in all the games this
happened, a single removal spell was played,

00:03:56.330 --> 00:04:01.659
the Zaxara player had no countermagic, and
just like that they and their pile of synergy

00:04:01.659 --> 00:04:03.800
permanents were rendered a total non-threat.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:08.120
I don’t wanna be too hard on this player
since it seemed like their deck was pretty

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:12.910
newly-built at the time and hadn’t been
tested much, but this is an example of a trap

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:16.950
that’s pretty easy to fall into if you’re
not careful and are just adding stuff that

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:21.690
looks good without thinking about the implications
of those additions.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:26.430
The other main type of payoff card is what
I’ll call conversion cards—ones that give

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:31.289
you one type of resource as a result of having
or spending a different one.

00:04:31.289 --> 00:04:35.530
For example, spending mana to draw cards when
you’ve gained life, drawing cards based

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:39.710
on having a bunch of creatures of the same
type, creating wolves based on how many cards

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:43.790
are in your graveyard, and creating angels
when you play enchantments.

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:48.710
As with amplification cards, these aren’t
inherently bad and are indeed a perfectly

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.250
solid type of card in moderation.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:56.320
But running too many of these resource conversion
cards will have problematic results for deck

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:00.860
consistency—if your deck’s core building
blocks already require you to be doing well

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:06.850
in some capacity, you’re limiting your ability
to get your gameplan going in the first place.

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:10.889
Pair this with running an excessive quantity
of amplification cards as well, and you’ll

00:05:10.889 --> 00:05:15.920
find that your deck will struggle to get into
gear consistently, will struggle to play through

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.780
interaction, and will struggle to get up off
the floor after it’s been shut down.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:24.069
Sure, you’ll have the crazy pop off games
from time to time where things line up just

00:05:24.069 --> 00:05:29.250
right, but the overall result of a high variance
deck like this is this sort of all or nothing

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:34.910
dynamic that I find very negative, and if
the whole table is playing this sort of deck,

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:38.880
differences in deck power levels will naturally
be amplified wildly.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:44.310
When one player gets the lucky pop-off whereas
the others get stuck with dead hands full

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:49.420
of unmet payoff cards, and especially when
nobody has interaction to close this sort

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:55.280
of gap in performance, decks can feel poorly
matched and power imbalanced even when they’re

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:58.480
the exact same power level.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:02.860
As a foil to these sorts of decks, I’m going
to dig back into my past for a moment and

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:06.139
talk about a couple decks I built a few years
ago.

00:06:06.139 --> 00:06:12.110
From 2019 to 2022, my playgroup, mostly made
up of broke college students at that point,

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:18.949
played exclusively with a pool of ten $30
EDH decks, and we agonizingly tuned and pruned

00:06:18.949 --> 00:06:23.610
and fully rebuilt these decks when needed
until each of these decks played to a power

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:25.630
we were satisfied with.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:30.460
The end result of this process was a pool
of decks that are not like most casual EDH

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:35.669
decks—these decks have been brought to life
in a setting that is almost competitive in

00:06:35.669 --> 00:06:40.400
nature, where there is a meta to keep up with
and adapt to, where each deck is played dozens

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.861
of times over with every single win and loss
tracked, and where decks that don’t make

00:06:44.861 --> 00:06:50.360
the cut, that don’t win a lot of games,
are scrapped without further question.

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:54.750
In this environment, decks are built with
a high degree of consistency and a lot of

00:06:54.750 --> 00:07:00.229
interaction, because these factors lead decks
to win more games in this sort of faux-competitive

00:07:00.229 --> 00:07:01.300
environment.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:06.759
This fact, paired with the low budget, resulted
in a format where resiliency and ability to

00:07:06.759 --> 00:07:10.389
grind were the most important factors for
a deck.

00:07:10.389 --> 00:07:14.539
The funny thing is, despite these decks being
built on a low budget and designed to play

00:07:14.539 --> 00:07:19.110
exclusively against each other, most of these
decks still hold up in games with decks that

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:23.680
are much more powerful on paper, and a big
piece of this is because of their relationship

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:25.370
with interaction.

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:30.390
Take a Dimir rogues list I built—it runs
six counterspells, six removal spells, two

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:34.949
modal cards that can counter or remove, and
three stax cards.

00:07:34.949 --> 00:07:39.360
In addition, the proactive cards in the deck
don’t rely on it being ahead or avoiding

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.530
opposing interaction—even something like
Bident of Thassa generates acceptable amounts

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:49.759
of value with a single evasive creature, of
which the deck is running 25.

00:07:49.759 --> 00:07:54.110
These factors work together to make a deck
that is very flexible, and which can perform

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:59.330
actions like hindering its opponents, chipping
in damage, and drawing extra cards even when

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:00.850
it’s behind.

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:06.250
Not only this, but the high watermark of the
deck is genuinely pretty high, with 30+ damage

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:11.220
combat steps, massive draw, extra turns, and
forcing opponents to discard their hands all

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:15.510
options on the table for the deck, and these
plays are important for allowing the deck

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:17.990
to get to the finish line once it’s a bit
ahead.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:23.289
A Rakdos discard deck from the same pool is
similarly heavy on interaction—it’s running

00:08:23.289 --> 00:08:28.150
ten cards plus the commander for removal,
as well as 25 cards that cause opponents to

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:29.320
discard cards.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:34.320
The deck’s cards also work well on their
own as a matter of necessity—when a deck’s

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.280
gameplan involves forcing the table into a
hellbent state, it’s not gonna play a bunch

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:42.490
of stuff that requires lots of setup because
that’s the exact sort of deck it’s looking

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:45.610
to disorient and hinder through discard spells.

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:50.190
Both of these decks are consistent, grindy,
and interaction-dense, leading them to fit

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:54.240
in quite well at a variety of different tables,
and always feel like they’re contributing

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:58.740
something to the game even when other decks
have significantly stronger cards.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:03.520
When I reflect on games with these old decks,
there’s something almost paradoxical at

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:04.520
play.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:08.220
These highly-optimized decks, decks that are
overwhelmingly dedicated to nuts-and-bolts

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:13.000
functionality over all else, led to gameplay
that was way more fun to me than a lot of

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:17.930
games with more conventional EDH decks that
have wacky card choices and perform lots of

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:19.760
big, splashy plays.

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:24.890
Playing decks that are packed with interaction
and also resilient to setbacks leads to fewer

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:29.810
feel-bad I’m-doing-nothing moments, and
way more moments where it feels like you’re

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:33.770
actually playing a multiplayer game where
different players are trying to navigate their

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:37.170
way to a win while keeping their opponents
in check.

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:41.600
And this is certainly not to say that playing
with a pool of hyper-optimized $30 decks is

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:46.380
only the way to achieve this—there are plenty
of things that people find fun about EDH that

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:51.200
won’t be found in an environment like this,
and there’s a reason my play group doesn’t

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:53.700
play exclusively with these decks anymore.

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:58.420
However, I think it’s worthwhile to consider
these sorts of decks as an example of how

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:01.110
EDH can be played.

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:06.030
We can think about EDH decks along a spectrum,
with the most interactionless, high variance

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:10.990
decks out there on one end of things, and
then decks like these which prioritize consistency,

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:15.399
resiliency, and interactiveness above all
else over on the opposite end.

00:10:15.399 --> 00:10:19.440
If you’re finding your games too frequently
feeling like a roll of the dice, where some

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:24.090
person happens into an insane opening and
then runs away with the game, just know that

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:26.500
swinginess is a malleable feature.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:30.290
You can move along this spectrum of gameplay,
and reducing the swinginess of your games

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:34.570
can be as simple as following the two deckbuilding
tips I’ve been mentioning in this video—running

00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:39.260
more interaction, and improving a deck’s
consistency and resiliency by removing payoff

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:42.470
cards that are unnecessary or difficult-to-fulfill.

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:46.040
Doing these things will generally reduce the
frustrating feeling of playing a deck that

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:51.190
feels like it’s doing nothing, and a table
with relatively consistent, interactive decks

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:55.460
will feel much more dynamic and balanced regardless
of how powerful each of those decks actually

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:55.470
are.

